# TEMPORARY: dynamic stepping stone for pinning the WhatsApp volume. Do not point the Deployment at this claim.
#
# The plan (see smart-home-docs/storage/PERSISTENT-VOLUMES.md, section 3c):
#   1. Apply this PVC. democratic-csi creates a zvol pvc-<uuid> and a subsystem k8s-services-whatsapp-mcp-data-nvme.
#   2. Write a marker file through volume-test-dynamic.yaml, then delete that pod.
#   3. On TrueNAS, snapshot the zvol and clone it to SSD_pool/k8s/block/pvc-whatsapp-mcp-data, then export the clone
#      through a subsystem named k8s-services-whatsapp-mcp-data.
#   4. Replace this file with a static PV (whatsapp-mcp-data-pv) plus PVC (whatsapp-mcp-data), as in
#      ../../../storage/postgres/pg-data.yaml, and keep this one as pvc.yaml.old. deployment.yaml already uses the final
#      claim name whatsapp-mcp-data.
# The suffix -nvme is deliberate: it keeps the dynamic subsystem name from clashing with the pinned one.
#
# 4Gi on purpose, so the pinned volume can be expanded to 5Gi as a test.
#
# WhatsApp session (whatsapp.db: the linked-device keys) and message history (messages.db), plus uploaded media.
# Losing this volume means re-pairing the phone. Block storage (ext4) rather than NFS, because both files are SQLite and
# SQLite locking is unreliable over NFS. The truenas-nvmeof class has reclaimPolicy Retain, so deleting the PVC leaves the
# data behind on TrueNAS.
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: whatsapp-mcp-data-nvme
  namespace: services
  labels:
    app: whatsapp-mcp
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: truenas-nvmeof
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 4Gi
